# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Module 9 expansion, part 2: after the patient leaves, a nurse's guide to HD medicines, and the routine laboratory panel.
OBJECTIVES = [
    "Assess readiness to leave the chair and teach post-dialysis safety, including recovery time, delayed low blood pressure and falls, access rebleeding, and low blood sugar, and report patterns that need prescription review.",
    "Check each hemodialysis (HD) medicine's purpose, timing relative to the session, and monitoring points against the order, and report findings without prescribing or changing a dose.",
    "Draw the routine HD laboratory panel correctly, read each result and its trend against the unit's targets and the guideline anchors, and flag abnormal values for the monthly round.",
]
EXTRA = [
    {"after": "unit-emergencies", "id": "post-dialysis", "nav": "After the session", "tag": "Clinical application",
     "h2": "After the patient leaves: the hours between sessions",
     "html": """
<p>The ending section covered the last minutes in the chair. This section follows the patient home. Hemodialysis (HD) compresses two or three days of kidney work into four hours, and the body keeps adjusting for hours afterward: fluid moves back from the tissues into the blood, urea and potassium rebound from the cells, blood pressure settles, and medicines held for the session are taken again. Most problems after dialysis are predictable from what happened during it, which is why the post-treatment assessment and the questions asked at the next arrival are one continuous piece of monitoring.</p>
<p><strong>Post-dialysis fatigue and recovery time.</strong> Many patients feel washed out after treatment, and for some the exhaustion lasts the rest of the day. The likely contributors are physiological: intradialytic hypotension (IDH) and a high hourly fluid-removal rate, which reduce blood flow to the heart and brain; rapid shifts of fluid and solutes; anemia; inflammation; poor sleep; low mood; and physical deconditioning.[[flythe2011]][[flythe2020]] A simple question captures it: <em>&ldquo;After a session, how long does it take you to feel like yourself again?&rdquo;</em> Ask it the same way each month and record the answer in hours. A recovery time that is lengthening is a finding to report, even when the adequacy result is acceptable, because it can point to a target weight that is too low, a removal rate that is too high, worsening anemia, an infection, or depression.</p>
<p><strong>Delayed low blood pressure and falls.</strong> A patient who stood safely in the unit can still become dizzy at home: on getting up from bed or the toilet, in a hot room or a crowded jeepney, after a large meal, or after taking a blood-pressure medicine that was held for the session. Older people, people with diabetes whose blood-pressure reflexes are damaged, and those finishing below their target weight are most at risk. Teach them to rise in stages (sit, dangle the legs, then stand), to sit or lie down at once if dizzy, to have someone nearby for the first trip to the bathroom, and to take held medicines exactly as the physician scheduled them. A fall, a near-fall, or fainting since the last session is asked about at every arrival, because it changes the fluid goal, the medicine plan, and sometimes the heparin order.</p>
<p><strong>Rebleeding from the access at home.</strong> Needle sites can reopen hours later, when the dressing is removed, the arm is used for lifting, or the patient also takes an anticoagulant or antiplatelet medicine. Teach the steps before the first session ends and confirm them by teach-back:[[talevski2020]] press firmly with clean gauze or cloth on the exact site for the time the unit advises, without peeking; raise the arm; never apply a tourniquet. If bleeding does not stop, or blood is spurting, keep pressing and go to the nearest emergency department, telling staff it is a dialysis access. A catheter dressing soaked with blood, a cap that has come off, or a damaged lumen is clamped (if a clamp is present), covered with clean gauze, and reported at once. Prolonged bleeding at home is reported at the next session: it may reflect the heparin dose, a narrowing downstream in the access, or a site used too often (Module 7).</p>
<p><strong>Low blood sugar in people with diabetes.</strong> Failing kidneys clear insulin and some tablets slowly, dialysis removes glucose when the dialysate contains little or none, and the session displaces a meal. Low blood sugar can therefore appear on the way home or in the evening. Teach patients to eat their usual meal on dialysis days, to carry a quick source of sugar, to check capillary glucose when they feel sweaty, shaky, hungry, or confused, and to treat it as their care plan directs. Episodes are reported, not managed by skipping insulin on their own, because the prescriber may change the dose or timing on dialysis days.</p>
<p><strong>Headache, nausea, and cramps at home.</strong> Mild headache and tiredness are common. A severe or sudden headache, headache with vomiting or confusion, or any neurological change needs urgent care; in a new patient it may be disequilibrium, which can develop in the hours after treatment. Cramps after treatment usually mean that fluid was removed fast or close to, or below, the true target weight. Patients are told that warmth and stretching help, that they should not take medicines for cramps recommended by friends, and that repeated cramps are reported so the target weight can be reviewed.</p>
<p><strong>Thirst.</strong> Strong thirst in the hours after treatment drives the next weight gain. It follows sodium more than habit: a salty meal on the way home, or a net sodium gain during the session, makes the patient drink. Teach salt reduction in food terms (Module 3), ice chips and mouth rinses, and a daily fluid bottle. Report persistent post-dialysis thirst with rising weight gains, because the dialysate sodium in the order may need review.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Problem after dialysis</th><th>Check before the patient leaves</th><th>Teach</th><th>Ask at the next arrival; report when</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Fatigue, long recovery</td><td>Episodes of IDH today; hourly removal rate; weight against target</td><td>Rest is normal; light activity on non-dialysis days; report worsening tiredness</td><td>Recovery time in hours; report a lengthening trend or a change in mood or sleep</td></tr>
<tr><td>Delayed low blood pressure, falls</td><td>Sitting and standing blood pressure; dizziness; walking; escort</td><td>Rise in stages; sit down if dizzy; take held medicines as scheduled</td><td>Any dizziness, fall, or faint; report every fall the same day it is learned</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access rebleeding</td><td>Dry dressing; thrill present; anticoagulants at home</td><td>Firm pressure, arm raised, no tourniquet; when to go to the emergency department</td><td>Bleeding at home and how long it took to stop; report prolonged or repeated bleeding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Low blood sugar</td><td>Glucose check where indicated; food available</td><td>Eat the usual meal; carry sugar; check and treat per care plan</td><td>Low readings or symptoms; report every episode</td></tr>
<tr><td>Headache, nausea</td><td>Symptoms at the end of treatment; blood pressure</td><td>Which headaches need urgent care</td><td>Pattern and timing; report severe or recurrent headache</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cramps</td><td>Weight against target; cramps during treatment</td><td>Stretching and warmth; no medicines from friends</td><td>Cramps at home; report recurrence for target-weight review</td></tr>
<tr><td>Thirst</td><td>Weight gain trend</td><td>Salt in food terms; fluid bottle; ice chips</td><td>Interdialytic weight gain; report rising gains with thirst</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The next session is the follow-up visit.</strong> Begin each pre-treatment assessment with the interval since the last one: &ldquo;How were you after the last session, that evening and the next day?&rdquo; Then ask specifically about dizziness or falls, bleeding at home, low sugar readings, cramps, and how long recovery took. Record the answers alongside the session data, so that the physician can see the link between what was done in the chair and what the patient lived through afterward.</p>
<p><strong>What needs prescription review.</strong> The nurse does not change the prescription but recognizes when it is no longer fitting. Report as a pattern, with dates: recovery time that is lengthening; dizziness or falls after sessions; cramps at home; patients repeatedly leaving below or above target; repeated prolonged bleeding; low blood sugar after sessions; and persistent thirst with large weight gains. The usual responses, all prescribing decisions, are reassessment of the target weight, a longer or additional session to lower the hourly removal rate, a change in the timing of blood-pressure or diabetes medicines, a review of the heparin order, or a review of dialysate sodium.[[flythe2020]]</p>
"""},
    {"after": "post-dialysis", "id": "hd-drugs", "nav": "HD drug guide", "tag": "Clinical application",
     "h2": "A nurse&rsquo;s guide to the medicines of people on hemodialysis",
     "html": """
<p>The medicines section earlier in this module explained why dialysis removes some drugs and how timing is set. This section is a working guide to the medicine classes a dialysis nurse meets every week. It names no doses: the dose, route, and timing are always those of the current order, and products differ in how they are given. The nurse&rsquo;s role is fixed across every row. The nurse checks the order and the patient, gives what is ordered at the time ordered, holds a dose only when the order or the unit protocol says so, watches for the expected effects and side effects, teaches, and reports. Nurses never prescribe, start, stop, or change the dose of a medicine.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>What it is for</th><th>Timing relative to the session</th><th>What the nurse checks or watches</th><th>What to report</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (ESAs), and oral agents that stimulate the body&rsquo;s own erythropoietin where prescribed</td><td>Anemia of kidney disease</td><td>ESAs are usually given at the session, into the venous line or under the skin as ordered; oral agents are taken at home on the prescribed schedule</td><td>Blood pressure before the dose; latest hemoglobin against the order; storage as labeled; the access, since clotting risk rises as hemoglobin rises</td><td>Rising blood pressure or new headache; hemoglobin rising quickly or failing to rise; access clotting; missed doses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Intravenous iron</td><td>Replaces iron so that red cells can be made</td><td>During the session through the circuit, at the rate and dilution for that product</td><td>Latest ferritin and transferrin saturation (TSAT) against the unit&rsquo;s withhold rule; signs of infection; resuscitation equipment at hand; observation during and after the dose</td><td>Flushing, itching, chest or back tightness, breathlessness, falling blood pressure (stop the infusion first); fever or infection before a dose</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active vitamin D (calcitriol and analogs)</td><td>Lowers parathyroid hormone (PTH)</td><td>Some are given into the circuit at the session; others are taken by mouth at home</td><td>Latest calcium and phosphate before an in-unit dose, as the order specifies</td><td>High calcium or phosphate; patient confusing active with nutritional vitamin D</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calcimimetics</td><td>Lower PTH by making the gland more sensitive to calcium</td><td>Oral forms are taken at home with food; an injectable form is given at the end of the session as its label directs</td><td>Latest calcium; tingling around the mouth or in the fingers, cramps, or spasm; nausea</td><td>Low calcium or its symptoms; palpitations; nausea that stops the patient taking it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phosphate binders</td><td>Bind phosphate in food in the gut</td><td>With meals and phosphate-containing snacks, including a meal eaten around the session</td><td>How the patient actually takes it (swallowed or chewed, with which meals); constipation; tablets taken at bedtime or skipped on dialysis days</td><td>High phosphate with a timing error found; side effects that stop adherence; cost barriers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Potassium binders</td><td>Bind potassium in the gut to lower blood potassium</td><td>At home on the prescribed days; separated from other oral medicines as the label directs</td><td>Potassium trend; constipation; swelling or thirst with sodium-containing products</td><td>Potassium still high or now low; constipation; swelling or rising weight gains</td></tr>
<tr><td>Blood-pressure medicines</td><td>Control blood pressure and protect the heart</td><td>Individual plan: some patients are told to hold a dose before the session or to take it at night</td><td>What was taken today; blood pressure trend before, during, and after sessions</td><td>Repeated IDH; dizziness at home; high pressure before or after sessions; doses being skipped on the patient&rsquo;s own initiative</td></tr>
<tr><td>Heparin (unfractionated or low-molecular-weight, as the unit uses)</td><td>Keeps the circuit from clotting</td><td>At the start and during the session as ordered; an infusion is stopped at the time the order states</td><td>Bleeding, recent falls, procedures, or dental work since the last session (ask before connecting); time to hemostasis; dialyzer streaking</td><td>Prolonged bleeding; clotted circuits; a falling platelet count with new clotting (a possible heparin reaction); a procedure scheduled soon after dialysis</td></tr>
<tr><td>Antibiotics given at dialysis</td><td>Treat access and other infections in a patient who already comes three times a week</td><td>As the order states, often near the end of or after the session because dialysis removes many of them; blood levels are drawn when ordered, usually before the session</td><td>Allergies at the chair; the time of the last dose; the fever and access response; level results</td><td>Fever persisting; new rash; ringing in the ears or hearing change; a dose not given or a level not drawn</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insulin and oral diabetes medicines</td><td>Glucose control</td><td>Plan for dialysis days set by the prescriber; meals and doses shift with the session</td><td>Dose and food today; glucose per protocol and with any symptom; medicines that last longer or should be avoided in kidney failure, such as some long-acting tablets</td><td>Low glucose before, during, or after sessions; very high readings; a diabetes medicine on the list that the nephrologist may not know about</td></tr>
<tr><td>Laxatives</td><td>Constipation, which also slows potassium loss through the bowel</td><td>At home as prescribed</td><td>Product contents: magnesium-containing laxatives and phosphate enemas accumulate and are avoided (Module 3)</td><td>Constipation lasting days; any magnesium- or phosphate-containing product in use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Analgesics</td><td>Pain relief</td><td>As prescribed; sedating doses before travel home are a falls risk</td><td>Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) use, including over the counter; drowsiness, confusion, twitching, or slowed breathing with opioids and nerve-pain medicines that accumulate</td><td>Any NSAID use, especially with remaining urine output; new sedation or confusion; pain that is undertreated</td></tr>
<tr><td>Water-soluble vitamins (renal vitamin B complex with vitamin C)</td><td>Replace vitamins lost into the dialysate</td><td>After the session on dialysis days, as prescribed</td><td>That the product is the renal formulation prescribed; high-dose vitamin C or general multivitamins bought separately</td><td>Products containing vitamin A or high-dose vitamin C taken without the nephrologist&rsquo;s knowledge</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Reconciliation each month.</strong> Patients on HD often see several physicians, buy medicines at different pharmacies, and change brands for cost. Once a month, and after every hospital stay or clinic visit, compare the unit&rsquo;s medicine list with what the patient actually takes: ask the patient or family to bring every bottle and strip, including those from other doctors, and go through them by name, strength, and how each is taken. Look for duplicates under different brand names, medicines that were stopped but are still being taken, doses that differ from the list, and medicines with no clear reason. Record differences and report them to the nephrologist; the prescriber, not the nurse, resolves them. Reconciliation done before the monthly round lets the physician act on it there.</p>
<p><strong>Over-the-counter and herbal products.</strong> Ask by category, because patients often do not count these as medicines: pain relievers, cold and cough preparations, antacids, laxatives, vitamins, &ldquo;kidney cleansing&rdquo; products, herbal teas and capsules, and supplements bought online. Pain relievers of the NSAID type can harm remaining kidney function and raise potassium. Some herbal products and food supplements contain potassium, phosphate, or aluminum, interact with blood thinners, or have uncertain contents and dose. Patients are not scolded for using them; the product name and label are recorded and reported, and the patient is asked to check with the nephrologist before starting anything new.</p>
<p><strong>Drugs removed by dialysis: the principle.</strong> Removal depends on the drug, not on the class. Small, water-soluble molecules that are not bound to plasma proteins and stay mostly in the blood are removed well; large, protein-bound, or tissue-distributed drugs are removed little. The membrane matters too, because high-flux and convective treatments remove larger molecules than low-flux dialysis. The practical rules follow: confirm timing against the order rather than a general rule, give a post-dialysis dose when it is due, report when a dialysis-day dose was missed or given early, and remember that an overdose or poisoning with a removable drug is one reason a physician may order urgent dialysis. Questions about a specific drug go to the prescriber or pharmacist.</p>
"""},
    {"after": "hd-drugs", "id": "monthly-labs", "nav": "Monthly laboratory panel", "tag": "Clinical application",
     "h2": "The monthly laboratory panel: drawing it right, reading it, and acting on it",
     "html": """
<p>Routine blood tests are how a dialysis unit sees what it cannot see at the chair: whether the dose is enough, whether minerals and blood counts are drifting, and whether an infection has crossed between patients. A result is only as reliable as the sample, and only as useful as the action it prompts. The nurse is responsible for the first and shares the second: drawing correctly, recognizing a value that needs attention today, and preparing the trends for the monthly round. Numeric targets below are stated only where the current guideline gives one; otherwise the target is the unit&rsquo;s, set by the nephrologist.</p>
<p><strong>Rules that apply to every routine draw.</strong> Pre-dialysis samples are taken at the start of treatment, from the arterial needle before any saline or heparin passes through it, or from a catheter after the lock and the discard volume are withdrawn. Never draw from a limb or line with an infusion running. Avoid hemolysis, which falsely raises potassium and phosphate: do not force blood through a small needle or syringe, fill tubes in the order the laboratory specifies, invert gently instead of shaking, and send samples promptly. Draw on the same session of the week each month, as the unit specifies, because results after the long gap between sessions differ from midweek results. Label tubes at the chair, with the patient present.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>What it tells you</th><th>Drawing it correctly</th><th>Guideline anchor</th><th>Nurse&rsquo;s action on an abnormal value or trend</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Pre- and post-dialysis urea (blood urea nitrogen, BUN) for urea reduction ratio (URR) and Kt/V</td><td>The dialysis dose actually delivered</td><td>Pre-sample undiluted; post-sample by the unit&rsquo;s slow-flow or stop-dialysate-flow method with UF off, before rinse-back; same session; accurate weights and time on the record (Module 6)</td><td>KDOQI 2015: target single-pool Kt/V of 1.4 per session, with a minimum delivered Kt/V of 1.2, for thrice-weekly HD;[[hd2015]] URR per the unit&rsquo;s target set by the nephrologist</td><td>Low result: check sampling, minutes delivered, blood flow achieved, access recirculation, and clotting before anything else; report with these findings; repeat as ordered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Potassium</td><td>Risk of dangerous heart rhythms; diet, binders, bath, and missed sessions</td><td>Pre-dialysis; avoid hemolysis and fist-clenching; send promptly</td><td>Per the unit&rsquo;s target set by the nephrologist; dietary advice is individualized[[ckd2024]]</td><td>A critical value is reported at once, with an ECG or rhythm check per protocol; check that the potassium bath hung matches the current order; teach diet and binder use; report low values too, since the bath may no longer fit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sodium</td><td>Water balance between sessions; a low value often means more water than salt was drunk</td><td>Pre-dialysis</td><td>Per the unit&rsquo;s target set by the nephrologist</td><td>Report low or falling values with the weight-gain trend; check the dialysate sodium against the order</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bicarbonate</td><td>Acid balance; chronic acidosis wastes muscle and bone</td><td>Pre-dialysis; fill the tube fully and keep it capped, because exposure to air falsely lowers the value</td><td>Per the unit&rsquo;s target set by the nephrologist</td><td>Report low or high trends; note poor intake and missed sessions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Calcium</td><td>Mineral and bone balance; effect of binders, vitamin D, and calcimimetics</td><td>Pre-dialysis; brief tourniquet</td><td>KDIGO 2017: avoid high calcium in adults; measure every 1 to 3 months on dialysis[[mbd2017]]</td><td>High: check calcium-based binders and active vitamin D in the order and report. Low: ask about tingling and cramps, especially on a calcimimetic, and report</td></tr>
<tr><td>Phosphate</td><td>Diet, binder use, and time on dialysis</td><td>Pre-dialysis; hemolysis falsely raises it</td><td>KDIGO 2017: lower elevated phosphate toward the normal range; measure every 1 to 3 months[[mbd2017]]</td><td>High: check binder timing by teach-back and the diet history, and report. Low: may mean poor intake; report for nutritional review</td></tr>
<tr><td>PTH</td><td>Parathyroid activity and bone turnover</td><td>Pre-dialysis; tube type and handling as the laboratory specifies; same laboratory each time where possible, since assays differ</td><td>KDIGO 2017: for people on dialysis, about two to nine times the upper normal limit of the assay used; measure every 3 to 6 months[[mbd2017]]</td><td>Report values outside the range and, more importantly, the direction of change across results; compare like with like</td></tr>
<tr><td>Alkaline phosphatase, if the unit measures it</td><td>Bone turnover (it also comes from the liver)</td><td>Pre-dialysis</td><td>KDIGO 2017: about every 12 months on dialysis[[mbd2017]]</td><td>Report a rising value, especially with a rising PTH</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hemoglobin</td><td>Anemia and the response to ESA and iron; bleeding</td><td>Pre-dialysis (post-dialysis blood is concentrated by fluid removal)</td><td>KDIGO 2026: in people on dialysis, ESA initiation is suggested when hemoglobin is 90 to 100 g/L or below, and hemoglobin is kept below 115 g/L in people treated with an ESA[[anemia2026]]</td><td>Report a rapid rise or fall, a value above the order&rsquo;s range (the ESA dose may be held per order or protocol), and any sign of bleeding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ferritin and TSAT</td><td>Iron stores (ferritin, which inflammation also raises) and iron available to the marrow (TSAT)</td><td>Pre-dialysis, before any iron given that session; interval after the last iron dose per unit protocol</td><td>KDIGO 2026, for HD: start iron if ferritin is 500 ng/mL or less and TSAT is 30% or less; reasonable to withhold routine iron if ferritin is above 700 ng/mL or TSAT is 40% or more; test every 1 to 3 months[[anemia2026]]</td><td>Apply the unit&rsquo;s withhold rule before giving iron and report; report a high ferritin with a low TSAT, which suggests inflammation or infection</td></tr>
<tr><td>Albumin</td><td>Nutrition and inflammation together; a falling value predicts worse outcomes</td><td>Pre-dialysis</td><td>KDOQI 2020 uses albumin as one part of nutritional assessment, not on its own;[[nutr2020]] target per the unit&rsquo;s target set by the nephrologist</td><td>Report a falling trend with weight, appetite, and recent illness; arrange dietitian review (Module 3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Glucose or glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c), if the unit&rsquo;s panel includes it</td><td>Diabetes control</td><td>Pre-dialysis glucose; note the time of the last meal and insulin</td><td>Per the target set by the physician; glycated hemoglobin can read falsely low on dialysis because red cells live shorter and ESA therapy adds young cells</td><td>Report low readings at once and high trends at the round; check that home readings agree</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg), hepatitis B surface antibody, hepatitis C virus (HCV) antibody, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)</td><td>Infection status for station assignment, vaccination, and detection of transmission</td><td>On the unit&rsquo;s schedule; labeled carefully; results filed before machine assignment</td><td>CDC: HBsAg monthly in susceptible patients; antibody yearly in vaccine responders, with a booster if it falls below 10 mIU/mL; HCV antibody every 6 months;[[cdc2001]][[hcv2022]] HIV testing per unit policy and consent requirements; schedule per PSN and DOH rules[[psn2024]][[doh2012]]</td><td>Any new positive is reported to the nephrologist and infection-control officer the same day (Module 7); track vaccine and booster due dates; chase missing results</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Critical values do not wait for the round.</strong> A result the laboratory calls critical, most often a very high potassium, a very low hemoglobin, or a very low or high calcium or glucose, is reported to the physician when it arrives, with the patient&rsquo;s current condition, and the action and the time are documented. If the patient has already gone home, the unit&rsquo;s procedure for recalling a patient applies.</p>
<p><strong>Read trends, not single values.</strong> One result is a point; three results are a direction. A phosphate that has risen in each of the last three months matters more than a single high value after a festive weekend. A hemoglobin that has fallen steadily may be bleeding or inflammation even while still inside the range. A Kt/V that dropped once may be a sampling error; one that drifts down over months suggests the access is failing or time is being lost. Before reporting, ask what else changed: a missed or shortened session, a hospital stay, an infection, a new medicine, a different catheter, a change of laboratory. Present each value next to the previous two or three, with the unit&rsquo;s target beside it.</p>
<p><strong>The monthly round.</strong> Many units review every patient&rsquo;s results with the nephrologist once a month. The nurse prepares it: results entered and compared with the previous months; out-of-range values and trends marked; the relevant context written beside them (recovery time, IDH episodes, missed sessions, weight trend, medicines held or changed, reconciliation findings); and pending items listed, such as overdue serologies or vaccines. During the round, record each decision and who carries it out. Afterward, act on the changes as ordered, explain them to the patient, and confirm at the next session that the patient understood. A result reviewed with no recorded decision is a gap in the record, and a decision that never reaches the patient changes nothing.</p>
"""},
]
